    "title": "What is a tornado watch vs. warning? How to stay safe in Florida",
    "text": "Updated March 12, 2026, 7:55 a.m. ETA tornado watch has been issued for parts of the Florida Panhandle as a line of severe storms approaches the Tallahassee area Thursday morning, March 12. The National Weather Service in Tallahassee issued the watch at 5:56 a.m., and it remains in effect until 9 a.m. for Franklin, Jefferson, Leon and Wakulla counties in Florida.Just over the state line, the National Weather Service office in Tallahassee issued tornado warnings for Thomas. Twiggs, Baldwin, Hancock and Washington counties.Forecasters say a squall line moving through the region could bring scattered damaging wind gusts of 60 to 70 mph and the potential for a few tornadoes.The March 12 storms come amid a week of active weather, including several tornado outbreaks in the Midwest, which have killed two in Oklahoma, six in Michigan and two in Indiana since last Thursday, March 5.Tornado warnings in effectTornado warning (confirmed) — East-central Twiggs County, GA — until 7:45 a.m.Tornado warning (radar) — Northeastern Thomas and southwestern Colquitt counties, GA — until 7:45 a.m.Tornado warning (radar) — Southern Thomas County, GA — until 8 a.m.Tornado warning (radar) — Northeastern Baldwin, southwestern Hancock and northwestern Washington counties, GA — until 8 a.m.What is a tornado watch?A tornado watch is an alert issued by the National Weather Service. It means that tornadoes are possible in and near the \"watch\" area.A tornado watch is issued by the Storm Prediction Center for counties where tornadoes may occur, and the watch area is typically large, covering numerous counties or states.If a tornado watch is issued in your area, the NWS says to be prepared and be ready to act quickly if the next level, a warning, is issued or if you suspect a tornado is approaching.What is a tornado warning?A tornado warning is a more serious alert, indicating that a tornado is either happening or is about to happen, and you should take shelter immediately.There are two types of alerts:Radar-indicated warning: Strong rotation is detected; a tornado may form or is in the process of forming. When a warning is radar-indicated, it typically means the radar has detected a rotating thunderstorm (called a mesocyclone) capable of producing a tornado. However, not all radar-indicated warnings result in an actual tornado touchdown.Confirmed warning : A tornado has been spotted or detected by radar through a debris signature.Regardless of whether the warning is radar-indicated or confirmed, it is critical to take shelter immediately, as tornadoes can form quickly and radar cannot always capture what's happening at ground level.What to do in a tornado warningA tornado warning means a tornado has been sighted or indicated by radar. Take shelter immediately.Go to the lowest level of a building: Move to a basement if one is available. If not, go to the lowest floor.Stay in a small interior room: Choose a bathroom, closet or hallway — away from windows, doors and outside walls.Cover your head and neck: Use a mattress, heavy blankets, coats or a helmet if available to protect yourself from debris.Avoid large open rooms: Gymnasiums, auditoriums and rooms with wide roofs are more likely to collapse.If you are in a mobile home, leave immediately: Mobile homes are not safe during tornadoes. Go to the nearest sturdy building or storm shelter.If you are outside or in a vehicle: Get to a sturdy building right away. If no shelter is available, lie flat in a low-lying area such as a ditch and cover your head.Stay sheltered until the warning expires: Tornadoes can strike quickly and with little visibility during storms, especially when rain is falling.Florida radarFlorida weather watches and warningsStay informed. Get weather alerts via text.Brandi D. Addison covers weather across the United States as the Weather Connect Reporter for the USA TODAY Network. She can be reached at baddison@gannett.com. Find her on Facebook here.",
